[Music]  the national conversation about race  includes the u.s military defense  secretary mark esper has effectively  banned the confederate battle flag from  bases and installations but that's only  part of the issue in a 2019 survey more  than half of people of color serving in  uniform said they had personally  witnessed white nationalism or racism in  the military  according to the defense department  around 17 percent of u.s  troops identify themselves as black we  spoke with several former active duty  service members of different races and  religions  about their experiences and as a black  woman i had to wonder okay  is this happening because i have ovaries  or it's because i have melanin  10-year army veteran mary tobin says  racism in the ranks  often isn't camouflaged  as a student leader at west point tobin  had to inspect cadet's rooms  you come in with a white glove and of  course you check for dirt  and she quickly found a red flag two  white men who were roommates  i walked in and there was a very large  confederate flag  i saw the flag i looked at them they  looked at me  and i could tell they were trying to get  a reaction out of me  that happened two more times and finally  um i had a discussion with them and told  them about how that felt for me as a  black woman from the south  coming into the room every inspection  and seeing that flag  it hurt me dearly now nearly two decades  later  the confederate flag is no longer  allowed at west point  but tobin says reminders of that painful  past remain  one example is the name of a barracks  named after general lee i lived in that  in those barracks  at west point there's lee barracks  there's lee gate there's lee  road there's robert e lee housing area  enough  just before bronze star recipient dana  patard graduated in 1981  he overheard two fellow cadets call him  the n-word  one of them ran after me and said oh hey  dana i am i'm so sorry  i said what are you sorry for i'm so  sorry for calling you  i was so shocked i thought i i  cannot believe that and then i took both  of them aside and said  we are two months from graduation west  point and you  still only see me as a  batard went on to carry the nuclear  launch codes for president clinton  and command all u.s forces in iraq it  makes me wonder well  what else are they saying when you're  not around or when i'm not around  and did you ever worry about that not  really because my assumption is  they probably did we've got to  collectively work together  if we're going to end this legacy of  systemic racism  and it's going to take more than black  people to do that for the first time  african-american officers lead the air  force and west point  but this photo taken last fall shows the  lack of diversity among the military's  top ranks  the chances of me making a fulbert  colonel or general become less and less  likely  as i look at my competition  and those who are making a selection and  really feel like  there's no one representative of me  captain george bates said he was passed  over for promotion  early in his 27-year air force career i  knew i had more work experience  time in service education  my caucasian competitor has struggled  with the air force fitness test  and was still promoted i was praised  for how i handled the bad news as if to  say they thought i would be aggressive  or disgruntled captain tobin faced a  triggering moment when she said  a white superior dressed her down with  his hand on his gun  and i said sir could you please remove  your hand from the trigger well  and he goes well why why are you afraid  of my hand on a trigger wheel is it  because you're from the hood  is this what you're you're used to  you're used to people shooting at you  but others believe there has been  progress over the years  like retired marine platoon assad and i  think that  race is one of the things that the  military gets right more often than  wrong in compared to  our civilian counterparts saad a muslim  met her husband  evan in the marine corps openness  diversity  inclusion those are all byproducts of  serving in the military because  you're bringing young adults from all  over the country  with their own personal experiences from  their corner of the world  and you're putting them together and  that's why they all  encourage young people to serve  diversity makes us stronger  can we be a better country yes but  america  is worth fighting for and worth serving  we asked to talk to active duty troops  the pentagon declined but in a statement  said it's taking a holistic look to  address diversity  and increase inclusivity in the ranks  adding these efforts  strengthen our military readiness and uh  anthony and michelle i just also wanted  to point out that  west point graduated its first cadet in  1877 that was henry  o flipper it was almost half a century  later before the next graduate would  leave west point and that was personal  hero of mine  benjamin o davis jr who of course would  go on to lead  the famed tuskegee airmen earning four  stars as a general in the united states  air force but throughout his whole time  at west point he was shunned so for four  years while he was at west point  no one spoke to him except in an  official capacity  and i can only imagine the grit and the  fortitude and the discipline  that you would need to complete a  program like that with no one  speaking to you in 2015 west point  didn't name a barracks  for general davis yes well still so hard  to believe that they have  barracks named assistant this is new  york  for general robert well more than just  the barracks it was  there's the gate there's like four  different things that bear his name at  west point and that being said um the  military though  should be credited because as as a  couple of those  people mentioned you know it is it is  fought for diversity  and affirmative action on many fronts  and in many ways it has been needed out  front  yeah yeah in this country yeah as but  still also  let me add something to that let me add  something to that uh the military  uh first desegregated its ranks in 1947  almost everyone we spoke to  said that they in some instances have  experienced  uh things that have happened to them  worse in the civilian world  the military reflects what happens in  the civilian world  at some levels uh but uh oftentimes they  experience  uh things in the civilian world that  they do not experience in the military  all right thanks vlad

